body.translated_text:
One is disrespectful toward the <s>dharmabhāṇaka</s> (preacher of the Dharma),
and delights in jealousy regarding the <s>dharmas</s>;
one withholds the <s>Dharma</s> like a closed fist,
and creates obstacles for those who seek the <s>Dharma</s>;
disrupting and casting away in various ways,
one does not teach the <s>dharmas</s> praised by the <s>Jina</s> (Victorious One);
one is constantly engaged in self-exaltation
and delights in disparaging others, being lazy.
These four <s>dharmas</s> are proclaimed by the <s>Jina</s>
for the decline of wisdom of the sons of the <s>Jina</s>;
having abandoned these four <s>dharmas</s>,
one should cultivate the four supreme <s>dharmas</s> taught by the <s>Jina</s>.

One does not pay reverence to those worthy of offerings;
one accumulates remorse toward others;
those beings who are established on the path to awakening [...],
one speaks ill-repute and infamy of them;
with deceit, fraud, and dishonesty,
one [...] and destroys others;
these <s>dharmas</s> [if] pursued [...]

the mind becomes confused [...] regarding the supreme <s>buddhabodhi</s> (Buddha-awakening);
therefore, abandoning these [...],
one [...] the supreme awakening, far distant [...];
[...] pursuing [these],
one [...] touches the supreme awakening, [which brings] peace [...]

They do not speak falsehood even for the sake of life;
they always speak words that are meaningful;
free from deceit and fraud [...],
they always regard beings with sincere intention;
those pure beings who have set out for awakening —
the <s>bodhisattva</s> regards them as teachers [...];
and one praises their virtues in the four directions,
having established the perception of them as teachers;

The arrogant one seeks <s>lokāyata</s> (worldly philosophy),
[seeking] families [...],
one hates the true sons of the <s>Buddha</s>, the <s>bodhisattvas</s>,
and speaks their ill-repute everywhere;
and [sūtras] not heard nor taught [...]
[...] these are proclaimed by the <s>Jina</s>;
for one possessed of these <s>dharmas</s>,
there is no growth in wholesome <s>dharmas</s>;
therefore the <s>bodhisattva</s> [...]
should abandon from afar these four <s>dharmas</s>;
pursuing these [virtues], far toward awakening,
like space from the earth, exceedingly far [...]

And in humility, with a mind like a dog's,
toward all beings [...],
content with the gain of the true <s>Dharma</s>,
pure in livelihood, established in the <s>āryavaṃśa</s> (noble lineage);
not accusing others of offenses,
not seeking [...] faults and stumbles;
and where one's intellect does not grasp,
one takes the <s>Tathāgata</s> as witness there;
'I do not understand — the <s>Jina</s> (Victorious One) understands [...]'
the awakening taught by the <s>Sugata</s> (Well-Gone One) is infinite.

Delighting in the <s>Buddhas</s> in all births; and hearing the six <s>pāramitās</s> (perfections);
seeing the <s>dharmabhāṇakas</s> (preachers of the Dharma) with a serene mind, always with reverence;
and for one who is always diligent, dwelling in the forest — therein one always finds delight;

children.translated_text:
When abused, one does not abuse in return; when struck likewise, some who are reviled do not revile others;
when quarreled with, one does not quarrel back; when angered, the patient one does not respond with anger.
And investigating the mind inwardly, one seeks tranquility, possessed of mindfulness.
